  "output": {
    "stories": [
      {
        "editor_spans": [
          {
            "body": "The full text of the Motherwell speech includes something the briefing the day before (Story 5) left out: an offer to devolve DWP employment support. That is the only concrete thing in it. Everything else has been said before, including the line that power 'was never supposed to stop at the doors of the Scottish Parliament', the regional empowerment pitch and the No10 North branding. The rest is meetings to design a model 'in the immediate months ahead'. Employment support is a power Westminster holds and Holyrood can't take for itself, so this is the one real incentive Burnham has to offer. It explains why he can talk as if Westminster and Holyrood were equals and still expect John Swinney to stay at the table.",
            "cites": [
              305,
              306,
              296,
              318,
              352,
              353
            ]
          },
          {
            "body": "The offer comes with conditions. Scotland would design the model 'shaped by Holyrood', but it 'has to be based on the Easterhouse principle', and 'if it's not based on that, it won't work.' So the power is handed to Edinburgh along with instructions on how to use it. That is the same top-down approach the speech criticises everywhere else. In fairness, this also goes against how I read the briefing. This offer is routed through Holyrood, not around it. The question is no longer whether Westminster will bypass Edinburgh. It is whether Edinburgh will accept a devolved power on the condition that it pushes that power further down to local level.",
            "cites": [
              306,
              310,
              311,
              312,
              313,
              238,
              366
            ]
          },
          {
            "body": "There is a gap in the speech's own account of what went wrong. It explains Ravenscraig and Leigh in terms of where decisions were made: 'hundreds of miles away', with 'nobody asked'. But the clearest success Burnham describes is not about local control. It is about ownership and spending. He brought Greater Manchester's buses back under public control, which let him order them from Alexander Dennis in Falkirk instead of the cheapest bidder anywhere in the world. That decision helped Falkirk because Manchester had money to spend and chose where to spend it, not because Falkirk was given more power. Pushing employability budgets and R\u0026D decisions down to regions does nothing about who owns a steelworks or a refinery. Those are the decisions that emptied Motherwell and Leigh.",
            "cites": [
              84,
              91,
              92,
              195,
              197,
              198,
              199,
              299,
              216
            ]
          },
          {
            "body": "The present-day versions of Ravenscraig get the least attention. Aberdeen is 'very much on my mind' and he'll have 'more to say in due course'. On Grangemouth he offers 'working with the Scottish government' on 'new investment'. The thirty-year-old closure gets a whole passage of personal memory. The losses happening now, across North Sea oil and gas and Scotland's last refinery, get a sentence each with nothing promised. Whether this approach is any different will show in what happens to those two places, not in redesigned job-centre services.",
            "cites": [
              108,
              183,
              184,
              185,
              81,
              82,
              83,
              86
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
